# G013 — Find PI to the Nth Digit

*v2. See `pi.md` for prose: not everything is a choreography —
InnateScript is a coordination language. Pure computation delegates
to the resolver's native capabilities; the choreography is the
context the value is used in.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Circle Area Within Tolerance

[circle-area @radius @required-precision
    IN ORDER TO
        pull π to the required precision as a native call
        compute area as a calc expression with agent-free terms
        and verify the area meets the caller's precision constraint

    @pi-value <- [pi {digits: @required-precision}]
    @area     <- [calc {expr: "@pi-value * @radius * @radius"}]

    @area
        where precision at least @required-precision
            -> return @area
        otherwise
            -> #insufficient-precision
]

---

## Verb Surface

[pi
    digits          {n}                       ;; -> String | BigDecimal
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: delegate to host native `where` no coordination needed

[pi {digits: @n}]
    IN ORDER TO
        hand the computation to the resolver's host-language backing
        because pure math with no agent interaction does not benefit
        from choreographic framing

    the resolver routes to whichever host implementation is available
    (Common Lisp's arbitrary-precision, Python's mpmath, Rust's rug, ...)
    and returns the computed value verbatim
]

The boundary of the language: InnateScript orchestrates computation,
it does not perform it. G013 marks the edge — anything that is pure
math with no coordination delegates to natives.

---

## Demo

[demo
    -> [pi {digits: 10}]
    ;; -> "3.1415926535"

    -> [pi {digits: 50}]
    ;; -> "3.14159265358979323846264338327950288419716939937510"
]
